The only thing that the tiles version definitely doesn't do well is
teaching you what types of monsters a blessed scroll of genocide will
get rid of.
The only thing that the tiles icons give away that they probably
shouldn't is the colour of certain potions if you discover them while
blind (iirc).  Also maybe the various grey stones, but their tiles
appearance may be randomised, I'm not sure.

Apart from that it's down to personal preference.  I don't exactly
feel disdain for the tiles, it's just that there's more options open
to you if you train yourself to the non-tiles versions (Un / Spork /
playing & watching games on NAO / taking part in dev/null, etc.)

The feel of the ASCII game is slightly less childish, I guess.  I also
like the fact that the look of the various monsters is left to the
imagination of the player; kind of llike the difference between
reading a book and seeing the movie, even if the movie follows the
book extremely accurately.  If you see the movie first, you're likely
not to mind what the characters look like, but if you've read the book
and formed a mental picture of the characters, the movie can be a big
disappointment even if it's well done.
I think this is why very few players who play roguelikes without tiles
like the tiles versions.  Even though the tiles are tiny, you still
get some of the same effect.

